from ./Config import Config
from ./Fonts import Fonts
from ./Layout import Layout
from ./Output import Output
from ./OutputSize import OutputSize

export anchor SvgOutput:
    description:
        How a composition is turned into SVG. The SVG is Emul's reference
        rendering: Emul computes the layout, shapes the text and draws every
        box itself, rather than relying on the viewer. The PNG is made from
        it, and the HTML is expected to match it, as described in
        @{Output}.

    files:
        Each rendered composition becomes one SVG file in the output
        directory, named after the composition, such as `MyComposition.svg`.
        The file is self-contained. It has no scripts, no external
        references and no dependence on anything installed where it is
        viewed.

    canvas:
        The SVG's width, height and view box are the output size described
        in @{OutputSize}, in px, so one SVG unit is one CSS pixel. A canvas
        background, if set, is drawn as a rectangle covering the canvas.
        Everything is clipped to the canvas.

    layout:
        Emul lays out the composition with its own layout engine, following
        the CSS rules for each property in its list: block, flex and grid
        layout, positioning, sizing and spacing. The result is a box for every
        instance and a position for every line of text, all in absolute
        canvas coordinates. The SVG then draws those boxes; it does not
        describe layout itself.

    structure:
        Each instance becomes a group element, holding its own drawing
        followed by its children's groups. Groups are written in drawing
        order, which is the order the children are written in, changed only
        by zIndex within a stacking context. Component and composition names
        are kept as id attributes, such as `MyButton-3`, to make the file
        easier to read; they have no effect on how it looks.

    boxes:
        - backgrounds
            A rectangle filling the border box, with rounded corners from
            borderRadius. A background image or gradient is drawn on top,
            sized, placed and repeated as backgroundSize, backgroundPosition
            and backgroundRepeat say, and clipped to the same rounded
            rectangle.
        - borders
            When all four sides match, a single stroked rounded rectangle.
            When they differ, a separate shape for each side. `dashed` and
            `dotted` borders use dash patterns, and `double` two strokes.
        - shadows
            Each boxShadow becomes a filter: the box's shape, grown or shrunk
            by the spread, offset, then blurred. The blur's standard deviation
            is half the CSS blur radius, as in browsers. An `inset` shadow is
            clipped to the inside of the box.
        - opacity
            An opacity attribute on the instance's group, so it applies to the
            instance and its children together.
        - transforms
            A transform attribute on the group, combining translate, rotate
            and scale in that order, around the centre of the box.
        - clipping
            `overflow: hidden` becomes a clip path matching the inside of the
            border, rounded corners included, applied to the children.

    images:
        An instance with the htmlTag `img` draws its `src` image, placed and
        cropped as objectFit and objectPosition say. Image files are embedded
        in the SVG as data, so the file stays self-contained when moved.

    text:
        Emul shapes every run of text itself, using the font files described
        in @{Fonts}, and handles spaces and breaks it into lines as set out
        in @{Layout}. textTransform is applied before shaping, letterSpacing is
        applied to the glyph positions, and textOverflow and lineClamp cut
        the text and add the ellipsis before it is drawn. textDecoration lines
        and textShadow are drawn as shapes and filters.

    fonts:
        How text is written into the SVG is set by the SVG fonts option, in
        @{Config} or on the command line:

        - outline
            Each glyph is written as a path. The SVG looks the same in every
            viewer and needs no fonts at all, but its text can't be selected
            or searched. This is the default. Colour glyphs, such as emoji,
            are expanded into one filled path per colour layer, or into an
            embedded image when the font stores them as bitmaps.
        - embed
            Text is written as text elements, and the font files are embedded
            in the SVG, reduced to only the characters it uses. Its text can
            be selected and searched, but the result depends on the viewer
            supporting embedded fonts.

    colours:
        Every colour is written in the SVG as hex. Colours given as `hsl()` or
        `oklch()` are converted first, since not all SVG viewers understand
        them. Colours outside the sRGB range are brought into it.

    notInSvg:
        HTML attributes other than `src` have no effect on the SVG, and
        htmlTag only matters for `img`.

    precision:
        Coordinates are written with at most two decimal places, which is far
        finer than a pixel.
